\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces System architecture of the proposed Multimodal Search and Rescue (MMSAR) evaluation framework. Visible RGB and thermal infrared video streams are synthesized from text prompts and Gemini video generation, followed by cross-modality thermal transfer, dual-crop ($640 \times 640$) spatial extraction into left and right viewports, video-level bounding-box annotation in Label Studio, and full-rate 24~Hz frame extraction. Curated sequences are partitioned into 240-frame episodes under a parent-video grouped split protocol (Option B, [TBD]) with anti-adjacency snippet shuffling to eliminate cross-tile spatial leakage. At the edge inference stage, parallel lightweight YOLO11n backbones process incoming RGB and thermal frames at 24~Hz ($T_s \approx 41.7$~ms), integrated via soft disjunctive late fusion ($s[n] = \max (c_{\text  {RGB}}[n], c_{\text  {Thermal}}[n])$) and evaluated across five lightweight temporal decision methods ($W = 5$ frames, 208~ms latency bound). Alert decisions are benchmarked across positive event detection, false alert mitigation, decision delay, and edge processing overhead savings.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:system_architecture}{{1}{2}{}{figure.1}{}}
